Which revision the timeline is parked on is not editor-local state. It lives in the same global store the rest of `reactor-browser`'s selection state uses, so an editor, a drive app and Connect's own chrome all observe the same value.

Two getter/setter pairs:

```ts
function useSelectedTimelineItem(): TimelineItem | null | undefined;
function setSelectedTimelineItem(item: TimelineItem | null | undefined): void;

function useSelectedTimelineRevision(): string | number | null | undefined;
function setSelectedTimelineRevision(rev: string | number | null | undefined): void;
```

The setters are plain functions, not hooks — call them from an event handler without violating the rules of hooks.

`TimelineItem` is a union of two shapes. `TimelineBarItem` (`type: "bar"`) is a change candle: `revision`, `timestampUtcMs`, `startDate`/`endDate`, raw `additions`/`deletions` counts, and the bucketed `addSize`/`delSize` fields typed as `0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4`. That bucketing is worth noticing — the render height is a five-step scale, not a raw count, so the bar is comparable across documents of wildly different churn. `TimelineDividerItem` (`type: "divider"`) is a labelled separator instead: `title`, `subtitle`, plus the same `revision`, `timestampUtcMs` and date fields.

Every field except `id` and `type` is optional on both variants, so a consumer reading `item.revision` must handle `undefined`.

Note the two values are tracked separately: the *item* and the *revision* are distinct pieces of state, and `useSelectedTimelineRevision` accepts a `string | number`, so it can hold a revision identifier the timeline never produced a bar for. Reading revision directly is the cheaper path when all you need is "which point in history am I rendering".

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/05-EditorsUI/01-ReactHooks.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._
